<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/advanced/modernism-and-postmodernism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The theory lesson gave you the lenses; this lesson applies them to the century's two great formal
    ruptures. <strong>Modernism (roughly 1910–1945)</strong> broke narrative form because the world it had to
    describe — industrial war, accelerating cities, Freud's unconscious, Einstein's relativity, the collapse
    of religious certainty — no longer fit the Victorian novel's confident omniscience.
    <strong>Postmodernism (1945–2000)</strong> inherited the broken forms and stopped mourning them: it
    treated all form, including modernism's, as material for play, self-reference and suspicion. Read
    chronologically, the techniques are answers to two different questions — and the differences are
    precise enough to analyse sentence by sentence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the break, the techniques, the play, and the line between them" widget="equationStep" config={modernCfg} />

  <h2>Modernism: form follows fracture</h2>
  <p>
    The pressure that produced modernism was a crisis of <strong>confidence in shared reality</strong>. Freud
    put most of the mind below the surface; relativity dethroned absolute time; the Great War showed
    industrial civilisation destroying itself; the city dissolved the village's legible social order. The
    Victorian realist novel — a narrator who could see everything and arrange it — began to look like a
    comfortable fiction. The modernist answer was to rebuild literature from the inside of consciousness out:
    <strong>stream of consciousness</strong> renders thought as it actually moves, associative, ungrammatical,
    looping (Joyce's <em>Ulysses</em>, 1922, compresses all this into one Dublin day — 18 episodes in a
    thousand shifting styles, each chapter staging a different technique; banned in the US until the famous
    1933 ruling); Woolf's <em>Mrs Dalloway</em> and <em>To the Lighthouse</em> braid several minds through a
    single day or decade, time flowing in "moments of being" rather than clock sequence.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second modernist discovery is that <strong>fragmentation can be organised by something older than
    realism</strong>. Eliot's <em>The Waste Land</em> (1922) is a collage — five languages, quotations,
    abrupt cuts — held together by what Eliot called the <strong>mythic method</strong>: a parallel to the
    Grail legend (and to Joyce's Odyssey) that gives the modern chaos a shape the modern world no longer
    supplies. Ezra Pound's slogan "make it new" names the programme; his Imagist doctrine — direct treatment
    of the thing, no word that does not earn its place — shows it in miniature. Add <strong>unreliable
    narration</strong> (Conrad's Marlow, the transitional figure; Kafka's bureaucratic nightmares) and
    <strong>montage</strong> borrowed from film, and modernism's wager is complete: difficulty is honesty —
    a form that is easy would be lying about the world it depicts.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> modernist difficulty is showing off. Nearly always it is an
  epistemological claim: if consciousness is associative, if time is subjective, if tradition is rubble,
  then linear, confident narration is false — and the fractured form is the truthful one. The difficulty is
  the realism.</p>

  <h2>Postmodernism: play, and suspicion of the big stories</h2>
  <p>
    Postmodernism's philosophical core is Jean-François Lyotard's definition — <strong>"incredulity toward
    metanarratives"</strong> (1979): the grand legitimating stories (progress, reason, the proletariat,
    Western civilisation) have lost their authority, leaving many small local stories. Its literary core is
    <strong>metafiction</strong>: fiction that writes about its own fiction-making. Calvino's <em>If on a
    winter's night a traveler</em> (1979) addresses a Reader whose book keeps restarting — every reading you
    do is both story and subject; Borges had already imagined infinite libraries and forking paths, where
    narrative's claim to single truth dissolves into branching possibility. Where modernism fragmented in
    anguish, postmodernism fragments in play — <strong>pastiche</strong> (imitating styles without the
    parody's satirical bite), <strong>intertextual game</strong>, <strong>irony about irony</strong>, and
    narrators so unreliable the text instructs you in its own untrustworthiness. Related branches:
    <strong>magic realism</strong> (García Márquez's <em>One Hundred Years of Solitude</em>, 1967 — the
    marvellous narrated as domestic fact) and Linda Hutcheon's <strong>historiographic metafiction</strong> —
    fiction that interrogates how history itself is written (Rushdie's <em>Midnight's Children</em>; and,
    most consequentially, Morrison's <em>Beloved</em>, 1987, where the ghost story carries slavery's
    suppressed archive: postmodern technique in the service of recovered memory — the best postmodernism is
    rarely "just play").
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Feel the difference concretely.</strong> Take the same kernel event — a man hurries through a
    city street to keep an appointment. Victorian realism writes it transparently ("Mr Bloom walked quickly
    toward the bridge"). Modernism renders the perceiving mind: sensory drift, interruption, the past
    erupting mid-sentence — the event matters less than consciousness of it. Postmodernism interrupts the
    narration itself: the sentence pauses to note it is a sentence, the narrator doubts the street exists
    outside the telling, another version begins. Three styles, three epistemologies — one event.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brian McHale's formulation gives the distinction its cleanest edge: <strong>modernism's dominant question
    is epistemological</strong> (how can I know this world? who is perceiving? what is certain?) —
    <strong>postmodernism's is ontological</strong> (which world is this? how many worlds? what is real
    <em>in</em> a text that keeps collapsing levels?). Modernism seeks depth beneath surfaces; postmodernism
    suspects the depth was always another surface.
  </p>

  <h2>How movements actually happen</h2>
  <p>
    "Modernism" was not a mood that happened to writers; it was built by infrastructure. The movement's
    machinery: <strong>little magazines</strong> (The Egoist serialised Ulysses; The Dial and Blast
    published and polemicised), <strong>salons and circles</strong> (Bloomsbury's interlocking households;
    the Paris of Joyce, Pound, Hemingway and Stein — Pound as the movement's editor-in-chief, cutting The
    Waste Land by half), <strong>small presses</strong> that took risks trade publishing refused, and
    <strong>manifestos</strong> (Pound and Flint's Imagist rules: direct treatment of the thing; a word
    that earns its place; rhythm as musical phrase, not metronome) that made experiment programmable.
    The postmodern generation inherited the playbook and globalised it: the Paris Review-style interviews,
    university creative-writing programs, and the Latin American boom's publishing networks that carried
    García Márquez worldwide. The analytic point for a literature student: aesthetic change is
    sociological — networks, institutions and publication economics are part of the form's history, and
    the Harlem Renaissance (Hurston, Hughes, Toomer) alongside the women modernists (H.D., Stein, Woolf's
    own press, the Hogarth Press) correct the myth of a purely male, purely European movement.
  </p>

  <h2>Two close-ups: the techniques on the page</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Eliot's refusal of consolation.</strong> "Prufrock" (1915) opens with the most shocking simile
    in English poetry: "Let us go then, you and I, / When the evening is spread out against the sky /
    Like a patient etherised upon a table." A romantic evening, compared to anaesthetised surgery — the
    simile performs the modern condition in eleven words: perception itself numbed, romance's consolations
    refused. Read formally (the theory lesson's lens), the poem's rhetorically grand question ("Do I
    dare?") never gets answered; the hero rehearses action and grows old rehearsing; the mermaids, famously,
    will not sing to him. Difficulty here is structure: an anti-epiphany poem whose form enacts paralysis.
    <strong>Joyce's last word.</strong> Ulysses closes with "Penelope": Molly Bloom's interior monologue,
    roughly 24,000 words in eight enormous sentences with no full stops — the punctuation of sleep,
    association and desire, running from "yes" to "yes I will Yes." Every modernist technique appears in
    one device: stream of consciousness, mythic method (Penelope, faithful and unfaithful at once),
    the ordinary day made cosmic. And the postmodern rejoinder is already visible in the gesture's
    extensiveness — a device that knows it is a device, stretched until technique becomes the subject.
  </p>

  <h2>The unreliable narrator, formalised</h2>
  <p>
    Since the syllabus names unreliability, give it its technical treatment — it is the era's most
    exported invention. A narrator is <strong>unreliable</strong> when the story they tell diverges from
    the story the text lets us reconstruct: their account is compromised by naivety (Huck Finn missing
    the slavery he describes), self-interest (Stevens the butler in Ishiguro's <em>The Remains of the
    Day</em>, whose dignity-obsessed account the reader slowly outgrows), madness, or design. The
    mechanism is a gap between two voices: the <strong>narrating I</strong> (who tells) and the
    <strong>experiencing I</strong> (who lived it), or between narrator and implied author — and the
    reader's pleasure is forensic, catching the tell-tale evasions and supplying what the teller conceals.
    Modernism engineered the device for epistemology (what can we know of another mind? Ford's
    <em>The Good Soldier</em> makes the reader the detective of a single catastrophic
    misunderstanding); postmodernism radicalised it until the whole archive is suspect — Rashomon-style
    competing versions (Rushdie's <em>Midnight's Children</em> narrates a national history its narrator
    admits is riddled with errors, then asks which error is truer than fact). The professional skill it
    teaches — reading <em>against</em> the telling — is also media literacy's core move.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The heavyweight critiques give you the debate's two poles. Fredric Jameson ("Postmodernism, or the
    Cultural Logic of Late Capitalism", 1984/1991) reads postmodernism not as liberation but as symptom: pastiche
    and depthlessness as the cultural form of consumer capitalism, history itself flattening into images;
    against this, Jean Baudrillard's <strong>simulacra</strong> (1981) — copies without originals, the
    hyperreal — push the suspicion to the point where "reality" disappears as a reference, a thesis
    postmodernism is accused of performing rather than analysing. Habermas counterattacked from the other
    flank: modernity's project (reason, emancipation) is incomplete, not bankrupt, and postmodern
    scepticism abandons the tools needed to criticise anything at all. The literary world's own answer came
    in David Foster Wallace's <strong>"E Unibus Pluram"</strong> (1993): a generation raised on irony was
    lonely inside it; his <em>Infinite Jest</em> and the "new sincerity" that followed attempt sincerity
    <em>inside</em> formal sophistication — irony's armour with a human face.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three scholarly strata connect this lesson to the unit's other topics. <strong>Global modernisms</strong>
    overturn the Europe-first story: the Latin American vanguardias, the Arabic nahda, China's May Fourth
    generation, the Harlem Renaissance — modernism was polycentric from the start, and comparative study
    (the translation topic) shows techniques migrating and mutating across languages. <strong>Textual
    criticism matters peculiarly here</strong>: <em>Ulysses</em> exists in historically significant states —
    censored serialisation, the 1922 first edition, Hans Walter Gabler's computer-assisted "corrected" text
    of 1984–86, whose editorial choices ignited a genuine scholarly controversy about what the novel
    <em>is</em> — theory and editing colliding in real time. And the <strong>world epics</strong> stand behind
    everything: Joyce's Odyssey is a demonstration that the oldest narrative patterns remain the modern
    novel's deepest resource.
  </p>
  <p>
    A reading order, for those who want the arc firsthand: begin with the accessible modernists (Woolf's
    <em>Mrs Dalloway</em>, Eliot's "Prufrock"), then the monument (<em>Ulysses</em>, with a reader's guide —
    it was built to reward guided rereading), then the postmodern turn in its clearest forms (Borges's
    fictions, Calvino's <em>If on a winter's night</em>), then the historical recoveries (<em>Beloved</em>,
    <em>Midnight's Children</em>). Read in that order, this lesson's abstractions become datable events on
    the page: watch omniscience dissolve, chronology bend, the narrator become suspect, and the text begin
    talking about itself — the century's argument conducted sentence by sentence, and the equipment you
    now carry is precisely what makes the sentences legible.
  </p>
  </p>
  <p>
    Open questions: is "postmodernism" over, or has it become the water we swim in (metafiction is now
    blockbuster television)? Can the new sincerity escape being read as another irony? And as machine
    generates pastiche at scale — the postmodern condition automated — what is left of the humanly made
    fragment, the authored voice? These are the live questions of the literature you will read next; you now
    have the vocabulary to see the debates inside it.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Joyce (1922); Eliot (1922) and "Ulysses, Order, and Myth" (1923); Lyotard, <em>The Postmodern Condition</em> (1979); McHale, <em>Postmodernist Fiction</em> (1987); Jameson (1991); Wallace, "E Unibus Pluram" (1993); Morrison (1987).</p>
